WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.743
When we look at the clients
that we're dealing with

00:00:06.768 --> 00:00:08.543
The key problems that they're facing

00:00:08.568 --> 00:00:11.378
Are around how do they maintain
their IT infrastructure

00:00:11.403 --> 00:00:13.292
While also adding value to the business

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:18.890
To allow those organizations to
focus their IT people on key skills

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.140
And key activities that
the business will value

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.726
They need to be able to
move their current workloads

00:00:24.751 --> 00:00:28.450
Into that cloud context
without a massive investment

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.560
When we looked at that seamless
transition without conversion

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.560
We wanted to have a
VMware-based cloud solution

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.278
And clearly IBM Cloud with its
bare-metal server offering

00:00:43.303 --> 00:00:46.251
Where we can run a complete VMware stack

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.760
Allows us to protect our client workloads

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:54.360
Onto a VMware platform with zero
conversion of their source virtual machine

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.290
And allow them to run it live in the cloud

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.680
And also revert back to an on-premise

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:01.680
After any incident that has happened

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.500
The entire experience is
completely seamless to the client

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.980
And ensures that they have minimal downtime

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.590
And minimum potential
disruption to their organization

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:15.700
In a disaster recovery solution

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:19.751
Typically clients would
have done big band tests

00:01:19.776 --> 00:01:22.637
Once or twice a year where
they look at bringing

00:01:22.662 --> 00:01:24.938
Large swathes of their technology live

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.076
To test that it's valid

00:01:27.101 --> 00:01:28.254
And then they'd shut it down again

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.577
Very time intensive, it was very invasive

00:01:30.602 --> 00:01:33.264
To the day-to-day operations
of most organizations

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.880
What our clients are now doing

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.040
Is on an ongoing basis

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:38.896
Moving workloads to the cloud

00:01:38.921 --> 00:01:41.604
And allow them to run for
a period of days or weeks

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.040
In a live context

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.700
And then reverting them back
to their original location

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.233
Treating the cloud presence

00:01:48.258 --> 00:01:51.284
As an extension of their
on premise environment

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:53.668
And that's giving them a
huge amount of flexibility

00:01:53.693 --> 00:01:56.136
About how they approach
disaster recovery planning

00:01:56.161 --> 00:02:00.004
And certainly on the
functionality of their systems

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.630
IBM as an organization have
being incredibly supportive

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.960
The consistency of the behavior,
the speed of delivery

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.430
The scale that we can
achieve with IBM Cloud

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.297
These are things we couldn't do previously

00:02:14.322 --> 00:02:18.694
And these are elements
of a complex IT ecosystem

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:20.793
That we're exposing to our clients

00:02:20.818 --> 00:02:23.094
In a way that wasn't possible previously

